+**A green build is not a rolled dev, so the tracker waits for the roll.** The build ends at the
+pin commit it pushes to KubeCoderDeploy's `main`, and Argo CD rolls that commit to `kubecoder-dev`
+afterwards. Once the build is green, `track_build.py` reads the pin line from its console and
+follows the commit into Argo CD. It reads the Applications from the Kubernetes API with the
+default `~/.kube/config` and asks git only of `/work/KubeCoderDeploy`, which this environment
+declares. The pin line names `config/prd/values.yaml` too, but `kubecoder-prd` tracks `prd`, not
+`main`, so only `kubecoder-dev` is waited on; prd rolls at promotion (Path 2). The tracker returns
+once `kubecoder-dev` is `Synced` at the pin commit or a later one, with no sync running and its
+health not `Progressing`. Exit `0` with the summary's `=== Argo CD follow ===` section reporting
+`kubecoder-dev` rolled **is** the roll: nothing is left to check by hand. A build whose pins
+`main` already carried pushes nothing, and the tracker reports dev's current state without
+waiting. Build-Main's `Write image pins` stage prints a handoff line on every green build, so a
+summary saying the tracker found none is a pipeline fault to report, not a rolled dev.
+
+Every other stop has an exit status of its own, and the summary's `Result:` line says what to do:
+
+- **`4`, deploy untracked** — the build is green, but a deploy repo it pushed to has no clone in
+  `/work`. Run the `git clone` line the summary gives, re-run the same command, and declare the
+  repo in `.kubecoder/config.yaml` so the environment's next start clones it.
+- **`5`, the roll failed** — the sync of the commit failed, the app carries an error condition
+  (`SyncError` …), or it settled `Degraded` or `Missing`. Fix the cause and push again, as for a
+  red build.
+- **`6`, not rolled by the deadline** — `--roll-timeout` (600 s by default, counted from when Argo
+  CD saw the commit) passed before the app rolled. The app's line is what Argo CD reports, not a
+  verdict on the workload: its health can sit stale at `Progressing` when a commit renders no
+  change and so brings no sync. The diagnosis tells the two apart (below); re-run the command to
+  keep waiting.
+- **`7`, Argo CD will not roll it on its own** — Argo CD had not seen the commit after
+  `--pickup-timeout` (120 s by default; it syncs on push only, so a lost webhook leaves dev stale
+  but green), or the app has no automated sync policy. Both are the operator's: a refresh or a
+  sync in Argo CD, or the push redelivered from the GitHub repo's Recent Deliveries; then re-run.
+
+On `5` and `6` the tracker writes a diagnosis beside the failing builds' console logs,
+`<--log-dir>/argocd_<app>_<sha7>.log` (`/tmp/track_build` by default), and the summary names it:
+the sync operation, the conditions, the resources the sync failed, the live resources that are
+not healthy with their pods and Warning events, and the `tf-presync-<app>` hook Job's log. On `6`,
+an app whose `Argo CD reports:` line has it `Synced` at the commit or a later one with no sync
+running, and whose "Resources not healthy" section reads `none: each is healthy`, runs the commit
+with only its Argo CD health stale; that section judges the kinds it lists, and a resource it
+names is the rollout that is stuck. When apps end differently, `5` wins over `7` and `7` over `6`.
+`1` is a red build, with nothing followed; `3` is an operational problem.
 
